  "text": "According to the most reliable information, roughly one‑third of the nation's territory—about 1.2 million square miles—can be classified as forested land. About a quarter of that timbered area is currently uneconomic or inaccessible, another half consists of young trees not yet ready for harvest, and the remaining 300,000 square miles holds harvestable wood. Each year an estimated 3,800 square miles are logged and 3,700 square miles are lost to fire. Planting new trees to replace these losses would likely cost around seventy million dollars annually, but natural regeneration can often fill much of the gap, though not always with the most desirable species. The overall timber outlook is uncertain and may become serious, with a likely gap between the exhaustion of existing stocks and the readiness of new growth. Authorities recognize that total loss each year exceeds natural growth, reducing the forest capital. To keep the forest industry sustainable, a balance must be achieved nationwide and within each region. Better data on current supplies, their distribution, growth rates, and potential second‑growth forests are essential, as is knowing whether future stands will mature in time to avoid shortages. Detailed examinations of logged and burned areas are needed to see if suitable species are naturally returning. Recently, several European nations completed comprehensive surveys of their timber resources and growth, using the results to manage forests for a continuous supply. Following a meeting of national and regional officials, a comparable nationwide survey was initiated."
